<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cuban Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cuban Revolution was the armed rising led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> and his 26th of July Movement against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulgencio-batista">Fulgencio Batista</a>. It began with the attack on the Moncada Barracks in 1953, and from 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> fought from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-maestra">Sierra Maestra</a>. Batista fled on 1 January 1959, and Castro's government soon allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> into a communist state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution.jpg" alt="Cuban Revolution" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fidel_Castro_and_his_men_in_the_Sierra_Maestra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Batista overthrew the emerging Cuban democracy in the coup of 1952. In the 1950s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> ranked among the more advanced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> countries by averages such as life expectancy, but the averages hid sharp disparities. The economy depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, nearly one-third of the labour force lacked full-time work, and only 15 per cent of the rural population had running water. Batista's regime kept close ties with organised crime, and was marked by police corruption, censorship and anti-communist campaigns that suppressed opposition with violence, torture and public executions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>, a young lawyer, first challenged the coup in the courts. When that failed, he and his brother Raúl led the assault on the Moncada Barracks on 26 July 1953. At his trial he spoke for hours in his own defence, ending 'History will absolve me', and was sentenced to fifteen years. Under political pressure Batista freed all political prisoners on 15 May 1955, and the brothers went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p>The yacht Granma left Tuxpan on 25 November 1956 with the Castros and eighty others and landed on 2 December. Batista's army attacked three days later; no more than twenty of the eighty-two survived to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-maestra">Sierra Maestra</a>. In 1958 about 12,000 soldiers, around 7,000 of them untrained recruits, entered the mountains in Operation Verano, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> defeated them. The Battle of El Jigüe in July 1958 is widely considered a turning point.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Batista left the country at the turn of the year, and on 2 January 1959 the army commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> ordered his soldiers not to fight. Castro reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> on 8 January. Manuel Urrutia Lleó took office as president on 3 January with José Miró as prime minister. After the government nationalised American property in August 1960, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> froze Cuban assets, severed relations and tightened the embargo in January 1961. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-pigs-invasion">Bay of Pigs invasion</a> of 1961, by 1,500 mostly Cuban exiles, was repulsed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> became Cuba's main ally, and the 26th of July Movement became the Communist Party in October 1965. Historians debate whether Castro was a communist from the start.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cuban Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…logistical support, suspended the constitution; his Bureau for the Repression of Communist Activities carried out wide-scale violence, torture and public executions, the murders mounting in 1957, with estimates of the dead ranging from hundreds to about 20,000, until the <b>Cuban Revolution</b> forced him into exile in December 1958.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Cuban Revolution</b> of 1959 fuelled emigration to the United States, Puerto Rico, Mexico, Colombia and Venezuela.</blockquote><a href="/religion/santeria">Santería · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Cuban Revolution</b> followed in 1959.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism">Marxism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Exiled after 1955, Perón moved further left, praised the <b>Cuban Revolution</b> and liberation theology, and in 1956 gave John William Cooke command of the movement in Argentina; Cooke presented it as “antibureaucratic, socialist, profoundly national” and in Cuba combined it with Guevarism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/peronism">Peronism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words answer the farewell letter that Che Guevara, a commander of the <b>Cuban Revolution</b>, left when he departed Cuba to spread revolution in the Congo and later in Bolivia, where he was captured and killed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hasta-siempre-comandante">Hasta Siempre, Comandante</a></li></ul></section>
